WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.984
A performance regression is a deterioration in a
relevant performance measure after a

00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:10.261
change. Functional tests can still pass. Lizhi
contrasts expensive whole-system tests with

00:00:10.261 --> 00:00:15.421
small-scale tests that provide earlier, more
localized feedback, and asks how to combine

00:00:15.421 --> 00:00:16.359
their strengths.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:23.161
Local execution time alone does not determine
user-visible response time. In a new fork-and-

00:00:23.161 --> 00:00:29.460
join example, two independent branches take 40
ms and 12 ms and then join. If the short

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.384
branch rises to 18 ms, the ideal parallel
portion still takes 40 ms.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:39.525
This simplified example assumes no shared-
resource contention or other path changes.

00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:44.904
Workload mix can also change the importance of a
component. A path used by a small fraction

00:00:44.904 --> 00:00:50.341
of requests may become important when that
fraction grows. An architectural model
represents

00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:55.719
call paths, synchronization, resource sharing
and workload so the local measurements can be

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:57.079
interpreted in context.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:02.062
The model alone is not sufficient either: a
coarse subsystem model can miss a changed

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:06.869
function’s service demand. The lecture therefore
connects fine-grained performance

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:12.204
observations to the broader architectural model.
Does a 50% increase in one function’s time

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:14.314
imply 50% worse end-to-end response?

00:01:14.314 --> 00:01:20.154
No. Frequency, critical-path position, parallel
execution, shared resources and workload

00:01:20.154 --> 00:01:25.130
determine how the change propagates. Measure and
model those relationships.

00:01:25.314 --> 00:01:30.421
The first step runs small-scale tests on
original and changed code and collects local

00:01:30.421 --> 00:01:35.287
performance observations. Keep the inputs,
environment and measurement boundaries

00:01:35.287 --> 00:01:40.333
comparable. Repetition matters because timing
varies with operating-system activity,

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.097
competing processes, warmup and other effects.

00:01:43.314 --> 00:01:48.384
The displayed method compares distributions
using a Mann–Whitney U test and Cliff’s delta,

00:01:48.384 --> 00:01:53.511
then uses the mean difference as a performance-
deviation quantity. Statistical evidence and

00:01:53.511 --> 00:01:58.637
effect size answer different questions; neither
should be replaced by a single noisy timing

00:01:58.637 --> 00:01:58.862
run.

00:01:58.862 --> 00:02:03.739
The slide illustrates repeated runs, not a
universal sample size requirement.

00:02:03.862 --> 00:02:09.355
When teaching or applying the method, report the
measurement protocol, distributions, effect

00:02:09.355 --> 00:02:14.668
direction and magnitude, thresholds and
uncertainty. A small p-value is not the size of
a

00:02:14.668 --> 00:02:19.981
slowdown, and a local distribution difference
does not by itself establish a user-visible

00:02:19.981 --> 00:02:20.637
regression.

00:02:20.862 --> 00:02:26.421
New scenario: an updated serializer is
consistently slower in isolated tests, but its
inputs

00:02:26.421 --> 00:02:31.918
are larger than the baseline’s. Correct the
comparison before attributing the difference to

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:36.389
code. Comparable workload is part of the
evidence, not an optional detail.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:41.992
A statistically detectable timing difference is
automatically a serious production problem.

00:02:41.992 --> 00:02:47.472
True or false? False. Consider effect size,
measurement conditions, the modeled execution

00:02:47.472 --> 00:02:50.736
path and workload, and the relevant user
requirement.

00:02:50.889 --> 00:02:55.968
The next step maps local deviations to the
higher-level modeled activities. The lecture

00:02:55.968 --> 00:03:00.814
illustrates structural matching using maximum
common subgraph analysis, followed by

00:03:00.814 --> 00:03:05.893
propagation up the relevant call structure so
the affected architectural service-demand

00:03:05.893 --> 00:03:07.411
parameters can be updated.

00:03:07.411 --> 00:03:12.471
A mapping must preserve the meaning and units of
the measurement. A function’s elapsed time

00:03:12.471 --> 00:03:17.531
may include waiting or downstream calls; it is
not automatically an independent CPU service

00:03:17.531 --> 00:03:22.536
demand. Record call frequency and nesting so the
same cost is not counted once in a parent

00:03:22.536 --> 00:03:24.482
measurement and again in its child.

00:03:24.482 --> 00:03:29.743
Keep original and updated model versions. Change
parameters supported by the local evidence

00:03:29.743 --> 00:03:34.947
and preserve other assumptions unless the code
or architecture changed them. Then evaluate

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:40.092
both models under relevant workloads and compare
the resulting performance distributions.

00:03:40.092 --> 00:03:45.411
The approach is useful for prioritizing costly
system tests, but a model is only as credible

00:03:45.411 --> 00:03:48.360
as its structure, mapping, parameters and
workload.

00:03:48.482 --> 00:03:53.060
Validate the baseline and use independent
measurements to check predictions. The

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.982
experimental results shown for TeaStore and
TrainTicket are evidence for those studied

00:03:57.982 --> 00:04:03.018
cases, not a universal no-false-alarm guarantee.
Can you add a function’s entire elapsed

00:04:03.018 --> 00:04:06.052
time to its parent’s demand without further
analysis?

00:04:06.052 --> 00:04:10.826
Not safely. The parent may already include the
child’s time or waiting. Establish

00:04:10.826 --> 00:04:15.895
measurement boundaries, call counts, resource
meaning and a mapping that avoids double

00:04:15.895 --> 00:04:16.426
counting.
